  "title": "Konica C35 AF",
  "subtitle": "The first mass-produced autofocus camera — a split-image rangefinder rebuilt in electronics inside a tiny point-and-shoot",
  "tags": ["Camera", "Autofocus", "Consumer", "Sensing", "Embedded Computing"],
  "blurb": "The Konica C35 AF (November 1977) was the first mass-produced autofocus camera. It took the C35 Automatic and replaced the mechanical split-image rangefinder with an electronic equivalent: two windows, a sensor pair, and a tiny computer that compared the two views and drove the lens to the distance where they matched. The user did almost nothing — raise, frame, shoot — while the machine performed the coincidence that had been the photographer's job since the rangefinder was invented. A cheap consumer camera in 1977, it is the moment auto-focusing stopped being a lab experiment and became a thing you bought.",
